# 🎯 PART 12 — Interview Answer: Code se Browser tak Complete Deployment

> Ye section specifically interview ke liye hai.
> Jab interviewer pooche: *"Ek naya microservice production mein deploy karna ho — code se lekar user ke browser tak — pura process explain karo."*
> Ye answer 3-4 minutes ka hona chahiye.

---

## Complete Flow — Ek Diagram mein

```
GitHub (code push)
    │ webhook trigger
    ▼
Jenkins Pipeline
    │ build → test → docker build → ECR push
    ▼
ECR (image stored with git commit tag)
    │ helm deploy
    ▼
EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) Pods (Running in K8s)
    │ ClusterIP Service
    ▼
Ingress Resource (K8s)
    │ AWS Load Balancer Controller reads this
    ▼
AWS ALB — Application Load Balancer (HTTPS — HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure :443, ACM — AWS Certificate Manager certificate)
    │ path-based routing (/api → api-service, /auth → auth-service)
    ▼
Route 53 (api.mycompany.com → ALB Alias record)
    │
    ▼
User Browser ✅
```

---

## Step-by-Step Interview Answer

---

### Step 1 — Code Ready

> "Developer ne code likha, feature branch mein push kiya GitHub par. Wahan se humara CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery) automatically kick ho jaata hai — GitHub webhook Jenkins ko notify karta hai."

---

### Step 2 — Jenkins Pipeline

> "Pipeline automatically start hoti hai:
> - Dependencies install hoti hain
> - Unit tests run hote hain
> - Lint aur security scan (Trivy) hota hai
> - Agar sab pass ho toh Docker image build hoti hai
> - Image ECR (Elastic Container Registry) par push hoti hai — tag hota hai git commit hash se taaki traceability rahe — kaunsi image kaunse commit se bani ye hamesha pata hota hai"

```
Image tag example:
  123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-app:a1b2c3d4
                                                      ↑
                                               git commit hash (first 8 chars)
```

---

### Step 3 — Kubernetes Deploy (Helm)

> "Jenkins phir Helm se deployment karta hai:
> - Pehle **Staging** namespace mein automatically deploy hota hai
> - Staging par smoke test hota hai — basic health check
> - Production ke liye **manual approval gate** hota hai — lead approve karta hai
> - Approve hone ke baad Helm production namespace mein deploy karta hai
> - `kubectl rollout status` se verify hota hai ki sab pods Running hain"

```groovy
// Jenkins mein approval gate
stage('Approve Production') {
    steps {
        input message: "Deploy to Production?", ok: "Deploy!"
    }
}
```

---

### Step 4 — Ingress + AWS ALB

> "Service ka type **ClusterIP** hota hai — directly internet par expose nahi hoti.
> Uske upar ek **Ingress resource** hota hai K8s mein.
>
> **AWS Load Balancer Controller (ALB Controller)** us Ingress ko read karta hai aur automatically ek **ALB (Application Load Balancer)** create karta hai AWS mein.
> ALB traffic ko path ke hisaab se route karta hai:
> - `/api/*`  → api-service
> - `/auth/*` → auth-service
> - `/pay/*`  → payment-service
>
> Ek hi ALB sab services handle karta hai — ek LoadBalancer per service banane ki zarurat nahi — cost bhi bachta hai."

```yaml
# Ingress resource example
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: my-ingress
  annotations:
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/certificate-arn: arn:aws:acm:ap-south-1:123:certificate/xxx
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/listen-ports: '[{"HTTPS":443}]'
spec:
  ingressClassName: alb
  rules:
  - host: api.mycompany.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /api
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: api-service
            port:
              number: 80
```

---

### Step 5 — SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) Certificate via ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)

> "HTTPS ke liye **AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)** se free SSL certificate issue hoti hai `api.mycompany.com` ke liye.
>
> Us certificate ka ARN (Amazon Resource Name) Ingress annotation mein add karte hain:
> `alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/certificate-arn: arn:aws:acm:...`
>
> ALB port 443 par HTTPS terminate karta hai aur pods tak plain HTTP forward karta hai — pods ko SSL handle nahi karna padta. Isse **SSL Termination (Secure Sockets Layer Termination) at Load Balancer** kehte hain."

```bash
# ACM certificate request karo
aws acm request-certificate \
  --domain-name api.mycompany.com \
  --validation-method DNS \
  --region ap-south-1

# DNS validation ke liye Route 53 mein CNAME record add karo
# ACM console mein "Create records in Route 53" button hota hai — ek click mein ho jaata hai
```

---

### Step 6 — Route 53 DNS (Domain Name System)

> "ALB ka ek DNS name hota hai — kuch aisa:
> `abc123.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com`
>
> Route 53 mein humari hosted zone hoti hai `mycompany.com`.
> Wahan ek **Alias record** banate hain:
> `api.mycompany.com → ALB DNS name`
>
> **Alias record** isliye use karte hain CNAME (Canonical Name record) ki jagah kyunki:
> - AWS Alias records root domain par bhi kaam karte hain (`mycompany.com`)
> - CNAME root domain par nahi chalta
> - Alias records free hain — CNAME lookup par charge hota hai
> - AWS internally resolve karta hai — faster"

```bash
# Route 53 mein Alias record banao (AWS CLI)
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id Z1234567890 \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "UPSERT",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "api.mycompany.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "AliasTarget": {
          "HostedZoneId": "ZP97RAFLXTNZK",
          "DNSName": "abc123.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com",
          "EvaluateTargetHealth": true
        }
      }
    }]
  }'
```

---

### Step 7 — Security via IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts)

> "Agar ye service AWS resources use karti hai — S3, SQS (Simple Queue Service), DynamoDB — toh hardcoded keys nahi use karte.
>
> **IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts)** setup hota hai:
> - Pod ko automatically temporary AWS credentials milte hain
> - Credentials har ghante auto-rotate hoti hain
> - Sirf wahi permissions hoti hain jo us specific service ko chahiye — least privilege"

---

### Step 8 — Verify karo

> "Deploy ke baad ye verify karta hoon:"

```bash
# Pods running hain?
kubectl get pods -n production

# Logs mein errors?
kubectl logs -l app=my-service -n production

# Health check
curl https://api.mycompany.com/health
# Expected: 200 OK

# ALB provisioned hua?
kubectl get ingress -n production
# EXTERNAL-IP column mein ALB DNS naam aana chahiye

# Grafana dashboard
# CPU, memory, request rate, error rate — sab normal?
```

---

### Step 9 — Rollback Plan

> "Kuch bhi galat ho toh rollback instant hai:"

```bash
# Option 1: Helm rollback
helm rollback my-service 1 -n production
# Previous Helm revision par wapas

# Option 2: Jenkins se previous build redeploy
# Jenkins → Job → Previous successful build → Rebuild

# Option 3: kubectl
kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-service -n production
```

> "Route 53 TTL (Time To Live) 60 seconds set rakho production mein — DNS change bhi fast propagate hota hai rollback ke time."

